# The test framework's own headers answer to the same self-containment rule as
# the headers under test.
package(features = ["parse_headers"])

# `benchmark/` holds a corpus and deliberately no target. It answers how fast
# rather than whether, and a duration needs a machine that is running nothing
# else, where a build system runs its tests in parallel and returns a cached
# result from whatever machine state produced it. It is driven by
# `tools/bench/run_benchmarks.py` instead.

# Three facts about a test are marked here, and they are independent.
#
# `no-remote-exec` says where it can run. A test that shells out to the host C++
# compiler spawns something that is not a Bazel action, so it runs on whatever
# machine executes it and finds that machine's compiler on PATH. The remote
# image has the platform's own C++ compiler and no clang, so a test whose
# answer is about clang is handed the clang of the release Lyra links
# (`@llvm//tools:clang++`, with its resource headers) rather than looking for
# one, and runs remotely like any other. That clang is the compiler and its
# headers and no more, so the tag holds a test that asks what a whole clang
# installation does on a machine that has one.
#
# `nightly` says when its cost is paid, and what it draws is one subject rather
# than one price: whether an emitted C++ project still builds and runs. Two
# targets carry it -- the whole corpus through that path, and the cases that
# assert what a project does once built. Both spend most of their time in the
# host compiler: the corpus compiles every unit of every case, and an emitted
# project carries its own copy of the runtime headers, which a prepared header
# cannot be shared across because it is bound to the paths it was made from.
# Both are therefore excluded for their own reason and not for a neighbour's,
# which is the test a removal here has to pass.
#
# Everything about the command line itself gates, because what a design
# declares and how a failure is reported are not statements about what a
# backend emits, so each case is a few builds of a small design.
#
# The two sides of `nightly` are complements, so every target is covered once
# and none twice, and a new one joins whichever side its tag puts it on.
#
# `cost` says what a test answers: what the compiler, or a program it built,
# costs as it is shipped -- time, stack, memory. A build that changes that cost
# on purpose, as the sanitizer one does to every frame and to the address
# space, would be measuring itself, so it leaves the target out.

# How many host compiles a test that drives the host compiler runs at once. A
# remote executor gives every test three cores whatever it asks for, so this is
# that many, and a test says so with `cpu:` too, which is what makes a run on
# this machine reserve as many as it uses. The test is told the count rather
# than asking the machine, because one that is running other tests beside it is
# not its own.
_HOST_COMPILES_AT_ONCE = 3

